<!-- FAQ -->
<section style="padding:72px 24px;background:#F5F0E4;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3.5vw,36px);margin:0 0 32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(22,51,44,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Poppins',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#16332C;margin:0 0 10px;">How do I know my mobile home needs leveling?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5F6E67;margin:0;">The usual signs are doors and windows that stick or swing on their own, cracks showing up around door frames, floors that feel bouncy or slanted, and skirting that starts to bow. Any one of these can have another cause, but two or more together usually means the home has settled. We check it for free, so there's no cost to find out.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(22,51,44,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Poppins',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#16332C;margin:0 0 10px;">How much does mobile home leveling cost in Ocala?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5F6E67;margin:0;">Most single-wides run $450 to $900 and most double-wides run $700 to $1,500. The big cost factors are how many piers need adjustment, whether any piers or pads need replacing, and how much clearance we have to work under the home. Every estimate is free and in writing before we start.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(22,51,44,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Poppins',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#16332C;margin:0 0 10px;">Do you charge extra for after-hours or emergency visits?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5F6E67;margin:0;">Yes, and we're upfront about it. After-hours and weekend calls carry a $150 to $250 trip fee on top of the normal job price, depending on distance and timing. Most people use the 24/7 line for pre-closing inspections or insurance deadlines where waiting until Monday would cost a lot more than the fee.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(22,51,44,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Poppins',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#16332C;margin:0 0 10px;">How long does leveling take?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5F6E67;margin:0;">Most relevels are done in half a day, and even a rough double-wide rarely takes more than a full day. We take water level readings before and after so you can see exactly what changed. You can stay in the home while we work.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(22,51,44,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Poppins',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#16332C;margin:0 0 10px;">Will leveling help me pass a 4-point inspection?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5F6E67;margin:0;">It's often part of the fix, but inspectors around here flag tie-downs and anchors just as much as leveling. We handle both, and we provide written documentation of the work that you can pass along to your insurance company or the buyer's inspector.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(22,51,44,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Poppins',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:800;color:#16332C;margin:0 0 10px;">How often does a mobile home in Florida need releveling?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5F6E67;margin:0;">In our sandy soil, plan on a check every 3 to 5 years, sooner if the home is newly set or you've had an unusually wet year. Homes on well-compacted ground can go longer. A quick inspection is free, so it costs nothing to stay ahead of it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

